# [DD-13b.W23.3] THE OPEN SUBTREE'S EXTENT — WHERE IT ENDS.
#
# `w23fix3_report.md` §5 named this fixture as OWED and said why the
# existing pair does not cover it: *"§9.1's plan has no fixture for an
# open subtree's own EXTENT (where it ends). `aux_malformed_body` probes
# an attachment error INSIDE the subtree, not the boundary at its foot."*
# §5.2a item 9's sharpest attack on the whole delivery is *"construct a
# file where the open subtree's extent under S1 differs from what
# `--list-source` reports"*. This file IS that attack, run by the
# delivery on itself.
#
# HEADLESS ON PURPOSE (see `aux_malformed_body.rxtin`'s own note): every
# line here is block-scoped, so all three legs answer.
#
# THREE POSITIVE ASSERTIONS, not one negative one, because an extent bug
# that SWALLOWS the next declaration and an extent bug that closes EARLY
# produce opposite symptoms and a fixture asserting only "the file is
# accepted" sees neither:
#
#   1. the `oracle` line after the aux body is a BLOCK DIRECTIVE, not an
#      aux row — the subtree ended at the dedent to column 1;
#   2. the `pattern b+` line below it is a BLOCK OPENER — the opener set
#      is live again outside the subtree, which it is not inside one;
#   3. `#section aux` (W23.4) holds exactly the body's own lines and no
#      more — the `m` line's key is spelled identically to one INSIDE the
#      aux body, so a subtree that ran on would claim it.
pattern a+
ext bench
  policy strict
  m this-key-is-spelled-like-a-case-kind
oracle pcre2
m "aaa" 0 3

pattern b+
m "bbb" 0 3
